### Reject a failed timer before waiting for messages

**Severity:** `medium`

`SetTimer` returns zero on failure, but the probe still enters `GetMessage` without a scheduled stopping message. A direct invocation can therefore wait beyond `DurationSeconds`. Check timer creation inside the existing `try/finally`, preserve hook cleanup on failure, and destroy only a timer that was successfully created.

```suggestion
        UIntPtr timer = UIntPtr.Zero;
        try
        {
            timer = SetTimer(IntPtr.Zero, UIntPtr.Zero, milliseconds, IntPtr.Zero);
            if (timer == UIntPtr.Zero)
                throw new InvalidOperationException("SetTimer failed: " + Marshal.GetLastWin32Error());
            MSG msg;
            while (GetMessage(out msg, IntPtr.Zero, 0, 0) > 0)
            {
                if (msg.Message == WM_TIMER && msg.WParam == timer) break;
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            if (timer != UIntPtr.Zero) KillTimer(IntPtr.Zero, timer);
```
